The task: refactor the duplicated "current processing result, operator, processed time" display logic across FormatItemJourney （事项查询）, FormatEntry （交接清单）, and FormatReport's 各项交接当前结果 （班次报告）. Centralize the business rule.

The rule: 
- If Status == EntryPending → operator="尚未处理", processedAt="尚未处理"
- Else → operator = whoIfRecorded(e.Operator), processedAt = fmtProcessedAtIfRecorded(e.ProcessedAt)

Note FormatEntry has a slightly different shape: "最后处理：尚未处理（等待接班人处理）；处理人=尚未处理；处理时间=尚未处理" vs "最后处理：处理人=X；处理时间=Y". The other two print "处理人=%s；处理时间=%s".

So I can create a helper like:

```go
// currentProcessing 返回交接单项当前处理结果的处理人与处理时间展示文本。
// 是否处理过以已保存的处理结果为准……
func currentProcessing(e HandoverEntry) (operator, processedAt string) {
	if e.Status == EntryPending {
		return "尚未处理", "尚未处理"
	}
	return whoIfRecorded(e.Operator), fmtProcessedAtIfRecorded(e.ProcessedAt)
}
```

Then use in all three places. FormatEntry can use it too:

```go
if e.Status == EntryPending {
	b.WriteString("  最后处理：尚未处理（等待接班人处理）；处理人=尚未处理；处理时间=尚未处理\n")
} else {
	op, at := currentProcessing(e)
	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "  最后处理：处理人=%s；处理时间=%s\n", op, at)
}
```

Or simpler: always compute op, at via helper, and branch only on the prefix:

```go
operator, processedAt := currentProcessing(e)
if e.Status == EntryPending {
	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "  最后处理：尚未处理（等待接班人处理）；处理人=%s；处理时间=%s\n", operator, processedAt)
} else {
	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "  最后处理：处理人=%s；处理时间=%s\n", operator, processedAt)
}
```

That preserves exact output. Also there's the result label difference: journey uses entryCurrentResultLabel (with 补充说明 for pending/returned/tracking), report uses Status.Label(). Those are intentional per-context wording — keep. The task says keep existing wording/order. So only centralize the operator/processedAt rule.

Also should I centralize the status label? The journey one has extra annotations; keep as-is.

Let me check tests to make sure nothing else. Also check service.go quickly for other display logic? The task says only these three places. Let me just do the edit and run tests.

